- How would you validate rotation can be performed with zero downtime?
- How would you handle compromised KEKs and re-encryption scope?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the candidate's ability to design scalable, isolation-preserving authorization systems in streaming architectures. It covers critical cloud-native concepts including per-tenant policy governance, ABAC vs RBAC trade-offs, lightweight in-memory caches, policy versioning, and safe propagation of live policy updates without cross-tenant leakage.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy governance and attribute-based access control (ABAC)
- In-memory caching strategies with topic-scoped isolation
- Policy versioning and cryptographic signing for auditability
- Secure propagation mechanisms that prevent information leakage

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo evaluator sketch
function evalPolicy(tenantId, resource, action, attrs) {
  const policy = cache.get(tenantId);
  if (!policy) return false;
  if (!policy.isCurrent()) {
    cache.refresh(tenantId); // async background refresh
    return false; // fail closed during refresh
  }
  return policy.evaluate({ resource, action, attrs });
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test policy rollback and maintain audit trails?
- How would you handle policy changes during active stream processing?
- What strategies would you use to minimize cache miss latency during tenant onboarding?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests stateful per-key windowing, dynamic rule handling, and exactly-once guarantees under late messages.

## Key Concepts
- Per-account sliding window sums
- Dynamic rule lookup and on-the-fly threshold changes
- Watermarks for late arrivals and eviction
- Exactly-once semantics and idempotent fraud_alerts
- Backpressure and test canary design

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: per-account rolling sum with 5m sliding window
state = Map(); // accountId -> [{ts, amt}]
rules = Map(); // accountId -> threshold
onEvent(tx) {
  cleanExpired(tx.ts);
  sum = sumAmt(state.get(tx.accountId));
  threshold = rules.get(tx.accountId) ?? default;
  if (sum > threshold) emitFraud(tx.accountId, sum, threshold);
  append(state, tx.accountId, {ts: tx.ts, amt: tx.amount});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle simultaneous rule updates and transactions to avoid inconsistent alerts?
- How would you scale to millions of accounts and high-throughput streams while maintaining low latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of access control in KCNA, multi-tenant isolation, and auditable, low-friction token rotation.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped ACLs and per-topic allowlists
- Token-based authentication with runtime rotation
- Non-blocking authorization checks and audit logs
- Backward-compatible ACL evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
// ACL data model and quick middleware example
const acl = {
  "tenantA": { topics: ["t1","t2"], actions: ["publish","subscribe"], version: 1 }
}
function authorize(tenant, action, topic, tokenVersion) {
  const entry = acl[tenant];
  if (!entry) return false;
  return entry.version === tokenVersion && entry.topics.includes(topic) && entry.actions.includes(action);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you store ACLs to support auditability and rotation without downtime?
- How would you test for token revocation affecting in-flight requests?
